Q: About C function, pass array defined directly in the function call line without prior array declaration A: <p>You can write a wrapper as for example</p>
<pre><code>int func_1_call( auto...values ) 
    requires (sizeof...( values ) != 0 ) &amp;&amp;
    ( std::is_same_v&lt;decltype( values ), int&gt; &amp;&amp; ... ) 
{
    const int a[] = {values...};

    return func_1( static_cast&lt;int&gt;( std::size( a ) ), a );
}
</code></pre>
<p>or like</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename ...T&gt;
int func_1_call( T...values ) 
    requires (sizeof...( values ) != 0 ) &amp;&amp;
    ( std::is_same_v&lt;T, int&gt; &amp;&amp; ... ) 
{
    const T a[] = {values...};

    return func_1( static_cast&lt;int&gt;( std::size( a ) ), a );
}
</code></pre>
<p>and call it like</p>
<pre><code>func_1_call( 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 );
</code></pre>

Q: Trying to write a simple program to test dup(2) and open() A: <pre><code>// Source - https://stackoverflow.com/q/47371628
// Posted by Jersey Fonseca
// Retrieved 2026-07-16, License - CC BY-SA 3.0

void redirection(char* file) {
    // Create file
    int file_desc;
    mode_t mode = O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_RDWR;
    file_desc = open(file, mode);

    //Redirect output
    dup2(file_desc, 1);

    // Print and close file
    printf(&quot;hello&quot;);
    close(file_desc);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Type not declared in implementation of function with CRTP A: <p>You need to qualify the names, because it's a dependent base class:</p>
<pre><code>void dosth_impl()  {
    using BaseClass = Base&lt;Derived&lt;Transform&gt;, int, 4&gt;;
    typename BaseClass::type out[BaseClass::size]; // &lt;-V use type/data from basic class
    Transform::calc(out, BaseClass::arr);

    std::cout &lt;&lt; out[0] &lt;&lt; '\n';
    std::cout &lt;&lt; out[1] &lt;&lt; '\n';
    std::cout &lt;&lt; out[2] &lt;&lt; '\n';
    std::cout &lt;&lt; out[3] &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre>
<p>The issue is that when looking at <code>Derived</code>, the compiler doesn't actually know what members <code>Base</code> defines, because it doesn't yet know what <code>Transform</code> is.</p>
<p>Notice that the base of <code>Derived</code> (<code>Base&lt;Derived&lt;Transform&gt;, int, 4&gt;</code>) depends on a template parameter (<code>Transform</code>), and even though <code>Base</code> is defined above it, someone could, in theory, specialize this template for some possible <code>Derived&lt;Transform&gt;</code> types to not contain <code>type</code>, <code>size</code> or <code>arr</code>. The compiler cannot assume these members exist in the base class, and as such, you need to explicitly qualify them - the unqualified lookup does not look where you want it to.</p>
